#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068680 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91a6a152-8dd3-5439-9d49-096c5284fcd4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Leg Given a Right Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1e14b9e-7fe9-592e-bd7b-579eb12ef45f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{12x+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e14b9e-7fe9-592e-bd7b-579eb12ef45f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{12x+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561986} :answer-field/id #uuid "9166490f-e949-5c2b-b086-c9c3c85b54de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a92ecba4-785f-5234-a5b9-74e110a01066" :question/math-academy-id "q-167515" :question/problem "Find the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the right triangle formed by two sides and a diagonal.\n\n![](images/ba/ba2ba6039f294df10db14d592ecc03f301a483745dfab2758a8fdc7df8a2b7d8.png)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(x+6)^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{(x+6)^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12x+36-{x}^{2}} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{12x+36} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4(3x+9)} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3x+9} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{3x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9a2ddbe-25aa-5681-9077-7c169747b5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9a2ddbe-25aa-5681-9077-7c169747b5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562054} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e2bf24-a108-5cef-9f88-dbe1fef86fa5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "152957b4-dc54-5769-bd44-bd7dec40a548" :question/math-academy-id "q-167516" :question/problem "![](images/46/4692bd00e1ba3f3db0d09da40ae2d85cf908c304185846c5dd0247ac6a2411c5.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the piece of land is $108$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze the given polynomial:\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd, and the leading coefficient (${a}_{3}=1$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n* $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n* $p(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ \n\nOnly the graph $A$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23c4516d-ba9c-5b55-b971-ceeb575d50c3" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Factor Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d56bde-124f-56bb-ab0f-be7499671792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4d56bde-124f-56bb-ab0f-be7499671792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561992} :answer-field/id #uuid "13985908-0030-5395-bdf1-39d1d4146009" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "def0feb0-2a39-52c6-9068-aa1b2c2978c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167517" :question/problem "Determine which of $x=6$, $x=9,$ and $x=10$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n{{field-1}}is a factor of $p(x)$" :question/worked-solution "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we consider the numbers $6$, $9,$ and $10$ as possible roots. Let's check each of these values in turn.\n\n* $x=6$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =(6)^{3}+3(6)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =216+108-972 \\\\  & =-648 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n* $x=9$ is a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =729+243-972 \\\\  & =972-972 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n* $x=10$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10) & =(10)^{3}+3(10)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =1\\,000+300-972 \\\\  & =328 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, $(x-9)$ is a factor of $p(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3c42bac-2253-5ceb-9b1f-45adfc8a61e9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial With Only One Real Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9102a43a-cdf1-5e62-84ba-e119681ed4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+12x+108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9102a43a-cdf1-5e62-84ba-e119681ed4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2+12x+108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561942} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03df3ec-0172-5e69-a59d-f8dd469d4012" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f871500c-ae9a-570c-8801-41a03ea03c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-167518" :question/problem "The polynomial $p(x)$ can be factored as \n\n$$\np(x)=(x-9)\\cdot q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial. Find $q(x).$\n\n$q(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To factor $p(x),$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-9)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-9)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-9)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $1$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-972$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $108$ | $972$ |\n|  | $1$ | $12$ | $108$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972}{x-9}=({x}^{2}+12x+108),\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-972=(x-9)({x}^{2}+12x+108).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+12x+108$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={12}^{2}-4(1)(108)=-288<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+12x+108$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $p(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n(x-9)({x}^{2}+12x+108).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12ce6eac-149a-5a2a-b5dd-6ca034848a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12ce6eac-149a-5a2a-b5dd-6ca034848a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561640} :answer-field/id #uuid "03dd5854-cdae-544b-93a0-890bf29fb831" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dfecd9e-e348-57b6-b46e-61f3cff328c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dfecd9e-e348-57b6-b46e-61f3cff328c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561779} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fafb0c-e45e-5efb-b68e-b2094c3fa0f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18714db5-7435-5dc3-be40-cc8ecbd5c18b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167519" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}+12x+108=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q$, and solve the equation.\n\n$p+q\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,x=-6\\,\\pm \\,\\text{i}$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+108 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x & =-108\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x & =-108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-108 \\\\ (x+6)^{2}-36 & =-108 \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =-72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=6,q=-72,$ and $p+q=-66.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & =-72 \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-72} \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-36\\cdot 2} \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm 6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-6\\pm \\text{i}\\,6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-6-6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54d9d8e4-c521-5b7d-bca7-f3c5b0d2b1d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e704852-6515-506a-bd4c-9aa898a46290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e704852-6515-506a-bd4c-9aa898a46290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186561783} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b844ac2-2039-543b-8af3-0016295b91da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a956a31-5964-5931-a8c8-e6aacda5b4d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167520" :question/problem "The magnitude of the complex numbers found in part $5$ equals{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's pick the complex number $z=-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$\n\nFor a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i},$ we have $a=-6$ and $b=6\\sqrt{2}.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|-6+6\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
